WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.040
Hello pals, it occurs to me that I've never really made a super quick introduction to my skill set.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.400
You know, just a fast video that respect my time and yours walking your way through my skills.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.840
We're going to walk through this documentation site, which is the docs for my skills.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.160
You can see they're nearly up to 220k stars.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.240
You can install them in two separate ways currently.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:26.480
You can either just get the Claude plugin just here or you can install the skills locally via NPX skills.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:29.360
If you want a really smooth managed install, then you use Claude code.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.640
If you want to tinker with the skills, then you'll install the skills via NPX.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:37.200
I've got about 20 minutes before I pick up my son and let's see if we can make that video now.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.160
And by the way, if you're interested in this stuff, then I'm releasing an AI coding crash course next week.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.680
The final pricing, the final release, will open on Monday.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.480
And so if you want to be first in line, then whoopoo if I just get out the way,

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.320
then you can join the wait list.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.720
This skill set is for engineering.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.360
So we are really focusing on engineering at the start.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:00.800
The main way you do engineering with this skill set is you first use the grill with docs skill.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.280
This is kind of a modification of the grill me skill, which is much more popular,

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:06.960
well, very popular down there.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.880
And the idea of the grill me skill is you align on an idea before committing to it.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.720
So this is really kind of like plan mode.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.840
And grill with docs adds just a little layer on top of that,

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.480
forcing you to talk about domain terminology and also add a little bit of documentation

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.080
on the decisions that you've been making.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.760
So architectural decision records.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.480
It interviews you pretty aggressively until you've sort of figured out what you're building.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:32.480
And then once you figure that out, you can then dive into implementing it straight away,

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:34.240
if you like via the implement skill.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.520
The implement skill, like most of my skills, is extremely small.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.600
Just implement the work described by the user.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:43.200
It uses a TDD skill, which is just really a reference skill.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.880
I'm getting it to do red green refactor.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.320
And then it calls code review to review the work at the end.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:49.920
The code review skill is really great.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.800
It does a dual access check.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.120
It first checks on the standards.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.240
So any documented coding standards in your repo will be picked up.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:00.800
And it checks against the original design.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:04.800
So this I found is super important for increasing the quality of what you put out there.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.000
So that grill with docs to implement that really the core.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.960
That's what you do on simple bug fixes, simple features,

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:15.520
where you can squeeze the grill with docs and implement into a single context window.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:18.720
And by the way, I don't mean just like one million tokens.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.000
I really mean the smart zone of the context window,

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.120
which inside my AI coding dictionary, you can see defined here.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.200
Early in the session, the agent is sharpened focused.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.920
As the session grows, it drifts into a dumb zone,

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.920
sloppyer forgetful, more mistakes.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.200
And the smart zone really is around 150K tokens.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:36.400
It's really not that big.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.440
I mean, your mileage may vary, but that's kind of my working metric.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.160
And so what happens when you have a task that you know is going to take more than 150K tokens?

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:47.360
What about 250K? What about a million tokens?

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:48.720
Well, for that, you do two things.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.400
You first define a spec.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.680
So you do your grilling session and then you define a spec.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.240
That spec is going to sit in your issue tracker.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:57.840
So that issue tracker might be GitHub,

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.400
might be beads, might be linear, Jira, whatever.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.640
And you can set up what issue tracker you're using

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:05.280
with the set up MacBook skills skill.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.680
So this one actually modifies your repo to say,

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.080
this is the issue tracker we're using, blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.160
So to spec, what that then does is it takes the grill with docs session

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:16.800
and it outputs a really nice specification

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:18.560
that you can use as a destination.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:22.000
The journey, the question of how you actually build the thing,

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.840
that is down to two tickets.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:26.080
So you have your spec, which is the destination,

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.480
and then each individual session of the agent that's building it,

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:32.720
each agent is going to get an individual ticket.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:34.720
So just like you would have an epic before,

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:36.880
where you would define what it is your building

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.600
and then you pass the tickets to the developers.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.840
Now you're basically saying, okay, we've got the spec.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.800
That's where we're going and the tickets go to individual agents.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:47.680
From there, each agent is going to implement that ticket

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:48.960
using the implement skill.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:50.640
So it's kind of like the same flow.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.560
It's just we've got a step to divide it up

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.000
over multiple context windows.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.120
So this is fine.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:59.760
This setup, when grill with docs can fit inside one session.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:02.640
But what about when you need to plan something absolutely

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.760
enormous, something that will grow outside one smart zone?

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:08.160
Well, for that, you can use the Wayfinder skill down here.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:10.000
So we're down to shaping now.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:12.800
The Wayfinder skill allows you to orchestrate

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:16.320
multiple context windows, kind of like to spec into tickets do

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:17.520
for implementation.

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:18.640
It does for planning.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:20.400
Wayfinder will grill you a little bit

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:21.680
on the thing that you're trying to build

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:25.040
and then create a map from which you can put tickets into.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:27.760
So it actually goes into your issue tracker as well.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:29.920
But these tickets are not implementation tickets.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:31.360
They're decision tickets.

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:34.560
So Wayfinder will say, okay, we need to decide about this thing first

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.320
and then we maybe need to build a prototype based on that.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.440
So it might use the prototype skill for that.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:42.240
It might then say, okay, we need to research something as well.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.720
We need like the external documentation for that

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:46.240
or we need to do a bit of market research.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:47.680
Then we can use the research skill.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:50.160
Once you end up with a proper Wayfinder map

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:52.080
where all of the decisions have been made,

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.720
all of the subissues of the map are closed,

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:56.880
then you go into to spec again

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:59.280
and you go through the tickets to actually build out the thing.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.360
By the way, I'm not really doing Wayfinder justice

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.960
in this section like Wayfinder is a massive skill

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:04.800
all by itself.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:06.480
And if you have any follow up questions to this,

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:08.200
obviously you can ask in the thread below.

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:10.120
But you can also just use the Ask Matt skill

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:12.840
which has information encoded in all of the docs,

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.240
but it's right there for you to use it.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:18.280
So you can actually ask it about your current situation

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:20.280
and which skill would be best useful.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:21.800
Best useful, most useful.

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:23.720
You can see I'm not spending much time on this video.

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:25.080
So that then is getting started.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:26.840
That is the main flow and that shaping.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:28.840
And then we have a few upkeep skills.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:30.160
The first two are pretty simple.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:32.960
The diagnosing bug skill and the resolving mode conflict skill

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:37.120
are basically trying to make the model better at things.

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:39.680
Most of my skills are designed to be user invoked.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:43.080
So the model doesn't even know about them until they pop up

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.640
which makes my skills very low on context load.

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:49.400
But diagnosing bugs, I found this gives a really great feedback loop

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:52.080
to the agent to basically say, okay, you're going to diagnose

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.840
a hard bug, you're going to create a rigorous feedback loop.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:56.440
Here are the steps I would take.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.200
So I found that models are just not very good at it.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.440
So that's my way of improving them

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:01.560
and it does a really good job.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:02.880
Resolving merge conflicts as well.

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:05.920
I was noticing that I was just getting some terrible

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:07.760
merge conflict resolutions from my agents.

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:11.560
And so this, what it does is it goes back and forces it

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:15.000
to look at primary sources to get blame really far back

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.640
until it understands why every change was made.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.080
If you've ever heard of Chesterton's fence,

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:20.760
this is exactly what this is doing.

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:23.760
You never remove a fence if you don't know why it was there.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:26.040
And that's what the resolving mode conflict skill does.

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:29.000
The triage skill is one that I've kind of gone away

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:29.840
from a little bit.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:31.280
I don't use it very much.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:33.480
But it's essentially a full triaging

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:36.760
any set of issues that you didn't create.

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:39.280
In other words, it's great for open source projects

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:40.280
where you just need to go, okay,

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:41.960
I've got a huge list of open issues.

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:43.440
How do I burn them down?

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.200
The triage skill is great for that.

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:47.200
The wizard skill is another great skill.

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:49.720
I was getting really sick of provisioning

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:53.240
like third party services and creating API keys

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:54.160
and all that stuff.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:56.080
And the wizard, what it does is it generates a script

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:58.520
that walks a human through the setup,

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:00.680
helping it as much as it can.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:02.640
And so this means that you can basically say

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:05.640
just create me a wizard to walk through provisioning

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.320
these 10 AWS services and it will do it.

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:10.280
It will open all of the URLs for you.

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:13.200
It will save the environment variables that you need to.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:16.160
It will help you as much as possible with the CLI itself.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:17.880
The wizard skill is such a lifesaver.

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:20.560
And then all the improved code based architecture skill

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:22.040
is such a nice one.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:23.800
This creates a visual report for you

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:25.600
so that you can look at,

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:27.360
you can basically point it at your repo

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:29.840
and just say improve code based architecture

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:31.440
in this specific area.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:33.280
And it will give you lots of candidates

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:36.560
for different ways that you can refactor your application

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:38.000
to improve your test suite,

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:39.880
to deepen your modules,

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:42.280
to make your code based just generally better

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:44.040
and easier to make changes in.

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:45.440
I could bang on and on about that skill,

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:46.600
but I've already got a video on it.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:47.480
We've got the Gromy skill,

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:50.440
which is the classic kind of like grilling without docks.

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:52.680
The hand off skill is when you want to hand off

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:53.760
to another agent.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:55.280
I find this really, really useful

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:57.960
because you can just basically just write a markdown file

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:00.000
if the thing you want that other agent to do

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.000
and it goes and does it.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:02.640
So to questionnaire skill is a great one.

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:06.600
This one is, it's a way of taking a grilling session offline.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:08.040
So when you're having a grilling session

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:11.000
and you realize, oh, I actually just need someone else's help

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:13.200
with this, you can take the grilling session

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.560
and you can turn it into a questionnaire.

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:16.960
This saves it as a markdown file

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:18.840
that you can then save into a Google doc.

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:20.640
You can walk through that questionnaire

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:22.520
with the person that you need answers from

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:23.920
and then feed that back to the agent.

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:26.600
The teach skill is just a remarkable little skill.

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:30.360
It creates HTML lessons to essentially teach you

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:33.480
anything that you can create a teaching workspace

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:35.920
where it's essentially trying to guide you

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:38.320
to learn that thing and learn the knowledge,

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:40.480
the skills and the wisdom that you need

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:42.280
in order to actually learn it.

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:44.320
And it's actually taught me a lot.

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:45.600
It's an incredible skill.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:46.840
I really recommend you try it.

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:48.520
It's amazing for curious people.

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:51.760
It's also absolutely nuts for onboarding onto co-bases.

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:53.840
You just say teach me about this repo

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:55.920
and it will create you a course,

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:58.320
a just in time course for that repo.

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:01.200
The WeightWatt skill is essentially an answer

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:03.800
to Opus 5 being incredibly verbose.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:06.320
You can get it, essentially use, you know,

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:08.560
the model spits out some garbage and you say,

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:11.120
WeightWatt and it goes and repeats it again

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:13.880
but this time a lot cleaner, a lot easier to understand.

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:16.560
And using the domain language that you created

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:18.400
in the grill with doc skill,

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:19.760
so it feeds into that too.

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:21.280
The writing for agent skill is similar

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:23.120
to the diagnosing bugs skill,

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:26.240
which is it's able, the model is able to invoke it.

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:29.320
And it's essentially a skill writing skill.

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:32.240
It's also able to work on your agents.md

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:35.480
or any documentation that is agent focused.

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:36.520
These skills are pretty popular

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:37.640
and people say they're well written

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:39.040
and the writing for agent skill

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:42.280
is basically my attempt to encode my skill writing skills

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:43.280
into a skill.

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:45.800
And then we have a layer of reference skills

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:47.880
that are used in lots of other skills.

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:49.720
We've already seen the TDD skill,

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:51.000
which is used in the implements.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:53.440
We've seen grilling, which is used in wayfinder

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:55.080
in grill with docs and grill me.

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:56.400
We've got the domain modeling skill,

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:57.920
which is used in grill with docs

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:00.360
and we've got the code-based design skill

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:02.800
which is used in various places.

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:04.800
Oh my god, I really tried to make this a short video.

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:08.200
I really did try, but I guess when you create 25 skills

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:09.240
and you wanna talk about all of them,

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:10.480
that's quite a lot of words.

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:11.560
But thank you so much for watching.

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:12.720
If you're interested in the stuff

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:15.200
then do sign up for the aircoding crash course

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:16.680
dropping on Monday.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:18.360
I will see you very soon.

